 trade in North America.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-gaultier-de-varennes-sieur-de-la-verendrye"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-gaultier-de-varennes-sieur-de-la-verendrye.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Gaultier de Varennes, sieur de La Vérendrye</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Gaultier de Varennes, sieur de La Vérendrye, was a French-Canadian officer, fur trader and explorer.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-laval"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-laval.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Laval</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Laval was a French politician who served as prime minister in the 1930s and became the leading figure of collaboration in Vichy France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-baud"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-le-baud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Le Baud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Le Baud was a Breton priest and historian in the service of the dukes of Brittany and of Anne of Brittany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville was a soldier and explorer born in New France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-mendes-france"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-mendes-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Mendès France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Mendès France was a French Radical politician and prime minister from June 1954 to February 1955.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-montet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-montet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Montet</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Montet was a French Egyptologist.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-nkurunziza"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-nkurunziza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Nkurunziza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Nkurunziza was a Hutu former rebel commander of the CNDD-FDD who became president of Burundi in 2005, after the civil war.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-pigneau-de-behaine"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-pigneau-de-behaine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Pigneau de Behaine</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Pigneau de Behaine was a French Catholic missionary and titular bishop of Adran who supported Nguyen Anh in his war against the Tay Son.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-poivre"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-poivre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Poivre</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Poivre was a French horticulturist and administrator who served as intendant of the Isle de France (Mauritius) and Bourbon from 1767 to 1772.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-rousseau-journalist"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Rousseau (journalist)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Rousseau was a French writer and publisher from Toulouse who founded the Journal encyclopédique in Liège in 1756.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-savorgnan-de-brazza"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-savorgnan-de-brazza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza was an Italian-born French explorer of the Ogooué and Congo basins.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-trudeau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-trudeau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Trudeau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Trudeau was a Liberal prime minister of Canada from 1968 to 1979 and from 1980 to 1984.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-werner"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-werner.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre Werner</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre Werner was a Luxembourg Christian Social politician who served as prime minister from 1959 to 1974 and again from 1979 to 1984.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon was a French socialist thinker and the first person to call himself an anarchist.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-paul-de-la-grandiere"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-paul-de-la-grandiere.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pierre-Paul de La Grandière</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pierre-Paul de La Grandière was a French admiral and governor of Cochinchina from 1863 to 1868.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/piet-cronje"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/piet-cronje.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Piet Cronjé</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Piet Cronjé was a Boer general of the South African Republic.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/piet-heyn"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/piet-heyn.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Piet Heyn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Piet Heyn was a Dutch naval officer and privateer in the service of the Dutch West India Company.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/piet-mondrian"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/piet-mondrian.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Piet Mondrian</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Piet Mondrian was a Dutch painter and a pioneer of abstract art.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/piet-retief"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/piet-retief.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Piet Retief</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Piet Retief was a Voortrekker leader from the eastern Cape Colony who led a party of emigrants into Natal during the Great Trek.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pieter-both"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/i